


At the first trail post, she checked the audio recorder and announced the time against the camera clock. Jonah held his phone beside the display. The seconds matched.




“Position one,” Maren said. “Entrance trail, blue route, first junction.”




Jonah pointed at the official map sealed inside her plastic sleeve. “The article puts the old settlement west of us.”




“The article’s red circle falls outside the preserve.”




“The coordinates do. The description doesn’t.”




“Then the description and coordinates disagree.”




He shifted the audio case to his other shoulder. “You don’t have to correct everything I say.”




“Then don’t use the article to navigate.”




They moved on before the argument could go further. Maren kept the camera low and filmed Jonah’s boots inside the trail edges. His bandaged hand rested against the case strap. He did not mention it.




The path entered the cottonwoods and descended. The air beneath the branches felt cooler than the prairie. The insect chorus changed from the dry pulse of crickets to scattered clicks and a continuous high tone from the low ground. Through openings in the trees, quartzite showed in red-brown shelves above the river corridor. The red-brown shelves remained visible after the soil and trunks had dimmed.




Maren stopped where the trail curved around an outcrop. She recorded thirty seconds without speech. Water moved somewhere below them, too far through the trees to see. A truck crossed a bridge upstream. Its engine reached them after the vehicle had passed between two gaps in the branches.




“Road noise,” she said for the recorder.




Jonah looked toward the river. “The sound last time came from the other direction.”




“Which direction?”




He pointed west, then lowered his arm. “West of the trail.”




Maren turned the camera to show his position and the direction he had indicated. “You changed that twice in the car.”




“I changed whether it came from the river.”




“You said west, then north, then behind us.”




“Because we were moving.”




She held the shot for another second. “Say that on camera.”




His expression tightened. “I just did.”




“Then say you weren’t sure of the direction because we were moving.”




He looked into the lens. “Fine. We were moving, so I wasn’t sure of the direction. The sound didn’t change.”




Maren lowered the camera. She could preserve the distinction. She could also show that he had supplied it after she challenged him. Both facts belonged in the record.




The shelter appeared through the trees as a dark horizontal shape. Jonah saw it first and walked faster. The trail widened into a clearing of packed soil and loose gravel. Rough stone pillars supported a low timber roof. Three sides remained open. A fireplace occupied the rear wall, its hearth filled with leaves, bottle caps, and pale grit. Graffiti had been scratched into the mortar and darkened with marker.




A metal plaque stood beside the path.




Maren set down her case and filmed the full shelter before moving closer. The stone looked older than the roof because rain had darkened the lower courses and lichens covered the north-facing joints. A picnic table sat beneath the center beam. The floor was concrete, cracked near one open side where tree roots had lifted the edge.




Jonah walked directly to the fireplace. “This is where the article says they found—”




“Wait.”




He stopped.




Maren aimed the camera at the plaque. The upper line read HARROW RIVER SHELTER. Below it, smaller text identified a state recreation project completed in 1936 and repaired twice after flood damage. A final paragraph asked visitors not to climb the masonry or disturb nearby cultural resources.




She read the construction date aloud.




Jonah came beside her. “That doesn’t mean there wasn’t a cabin here before.”




“The article calls this building the cabin.”




“It uses a photograph of this building.”




“It says the killings happened inside it.”




“It says this is the location people associate with them.”




Maren took the folded printout from her map case. The article’s photograph showed the same fireplace, though someone had cropped out the plaque and most of the open sides. Beneath it, a caption called the structure an isolated nineteenth-century cabin.




She held the printout beside the plaque and filmed both.




Jonah turned away. “The manager already said the page combines places.”




“Yes.”




“You don’t need to spend the whole episode correcting me.”




“The building is one of the reasons we came.”




“The sound is why we came.”




“When we heard the sound, you thought this was the building.”




He faced her again. “You heard it too.”




“I heard a long call. We did not record it, and neither of us located it.”




His voice rose enough to carry beneath the roof. “You said it didn’t sound human.”




“I said I could not make out words.”




“That is not what you said in the car.”




Maren kept the camera on him. “Then the recording from the car will show what I said.”




His eyes moved to the tally light. He looked past it toward her.




Maren stopped recording.




Neither spoke for several seconds. The clearing held the cricket pulse from the trail, water through the trees, and an intermittent tapping from a branch against the shelter roof. The sounds were easy to separate while they stood still.




“I’m not using the private card without your consent,” she said.




“You keep using it to win arguments.”




Maren did not answer.




“You remember the parts that support you.”




She let that stand.




A mosquito landed on the back of his bandaged hand. He shook it off.




Maren restarted the camera and turned it toward herself. The shelter stood over her left shoulder. She framed the plaque at the edge of the shot.




“Harrow River Preserve identifies this structure as a public shelter completed in 1936,” she said. “An online article used a cropped photograph of the shelter and connected it to a crime that occurred elsewhere in the wider area. The preserve manager told us that the article also combines settlement remnants, quarry features, and protected archaeological areas into one location. We have no official record of a grave or a nineteenth-century cabin at this shelter.”




She stopped.




Jonah stood outside the frame. “And local reports?”




Maren started again. “Visitors and paranormal investigators have reported cries, movement, and the feeling of being watched near the shelter and along the river trails. Those reports do not establish what produced the sound we heard during our scouting visit.”




She lowered the camera. “Your version.”




He stepped into position. He took a breath, looked toward the fireplace, and began with the version he had rehearsed in the office.




“For years, people have come to this part of Harrow River because of a story the official map doesn’t tell.”




Maren did not interrupt.




He described a cabin, a grave, and a presence that warned visitors away. He did not name a victim or repeat the article’s crime details. When he reached the shelter’s construction date, he said the present structure might have replaced or marked something older.




Maren kept the full take. When he finished, she recorded the plaque again.
